WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
One-hot encoding.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.561
Models can't do math on strings, so categories must become numbers.

3
00:00:06.561 --> 00:00:07.833
The question is how.

4
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:14.833
The naive fix is to number the categories: Kepler one, TESS two, JWST three.

5
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:17.108
But that invents an order.

6
00:00:17.108 --> 00:00:19.767
Is JWST somehow three times Kepler?

7
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.193
One-hot encoding avoids this.

8
00:00:22.193 --> 00:00:25.567
Each category gets its own zero or one column.

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:30.333
There is no false ordering, and linear models learn a separate weight per category.

10
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:32.900
Here is a small table.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.900
The first row says kepler, so only the kepler column is a one.

12
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.230
The third row says jwst, so only the jwst column is hot.

13
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.733
Exactly one column is hot in every row.

14
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:49.367
There is a breaking point: high cardinality.

15
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:55.300
One thousand cities would mean a thousand nearly empty columns, each with almost no examples.

16
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.100
Then reach for target encoding, which replaces each category with its historical target mean.

17
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.400
Or for learned embeddings, which come later in the course.

18
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.367
In pandas, it is one call.

19
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:11.067
We start with a telescope name column and a radius column.

20
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:16.800
Get dummies replaces the text column with one column per telescope, and leaves the number alone.

21
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.800
Here is the exoplanet case.

22
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.700
One-hot on telescope, with a handful of values, works great.

23
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.897
But discovery facility has over three hundred observatories, most with fewer than five planets.

24
00:01:29.897 --> 00:01:33.000
One-hot would give three hundred columns of noise.

25
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.864
So that one column gets target encoding.

26
00:01:35.864 --> 00:01:39.133
Encoding is a per-column choice, not a global rule.

27
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.133
Let's recap.

28
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:43.333
Models need numbers, not strings.

29
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:47.467
One-hot gives each category its own zero or one column.

30
00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:49.367
It adds no false ordering.

31
00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:52.803
For high cardinality, switch to target encoding.

32
00:01:52.803 --> 00:01:54.567
Next up: linear regression.
